**Read:** [Engineering Statics — Baker and Haynes](../sources/Engineering-Statics-Baker-and-Haynes.lingua#r_3DXGKGQSPPA9YDNH2WSB8XRN80), problem solving and rigid-body equilibrium; the opening elastic-response module in [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY). Compare the questions each model can answer.

A particle neglects shape, a rigid body neglects deformation, and a deformable body accounts for changes of shape. A static model assumes the relevant acceleration is zero; a slowly changing arrangement may justify a sequence of static states only if its inertial and rate-dependent effects are small enough for the question.

**Work:** Model the same lever first as a rigid body and then as a bending member. Explain why the first can predict reactions but cannot establish deflection or stress concentration. State the system boundary, support assumptions, loads, geometry, material behavior and quantities being predicted.

Test whether an assumption matters by changing it or comparing a limiting case. Model detail is useful when it resolves an important question; a detailed picture with unsupported boundary conditions can still give an incorrect answer. Preserve the difference between a numerical solution and evidence about a real machine.

